ldapdomaindump -u PROJECT\\hecscow -p Whyyouwannaknow4\@ 192.168.204.128

The final scenario involved setting up a lab to emulate a Kerberos exploitation attack, specifically AS-REP Roasting, 
which is subtechnique T1558.004 under T1558: Steal or Forge Kerberos Tickets. AS-REP Roasting targets Kerberos authentication 
in Active Directory environments where pre-authentication is disabled for user accounts, allowing attackers to request and receive 
encrypted ticket responses that can be cracked offline for password hashes. This technique exploits the Authentication Server Response (AS-REP) 
message in the Kerberos protocol. Adversaries use it to harvest credentials without needing initial valid authentication, enabling them to 
compromise accounts stealthily, especially in misconfigured domains, and use the obtained hashes for further attacks like pass-the-hash or overpass-the-hash.

In this attack, an attacker enumerates accounts with pre-auth disabled, sends an AS-REQ without pre-auth data, receives the AS-REP, extracts the hash, 
and cracks it offline. Prerequisites include domain access (even low-privileged) and vulnerable accounts. The lab setup included a Windows Domain Controller VM 
and victim user accounts configured without pre-auth.
